{
 "result": "Built lcs.py: prints the LCS length of two stdin lines. It loads a compiled C helper (lcs_c.so, built from lcs_c.c) exposing lcs_len(), which computes the bit-parallel LCS (Hyyr\u00f6/Bit-Parallel formulation: per-character bitmask M over the shorter string, V = x & ~(x - ((V<<1)|1)) with x = M|V, answer = popcount(V)), with a pure-Python fallback (_py_lcs, same algorithm) if the shared library cannot be loaded. Correctness is checked by assay.py, which compares lcs_length() against a plain O(nm) DP on 14 small/medium cases (including empty strings, identical strings, disjoint alphabets, binary/NUL-containing data), runs structural checks at full 3000-char size (string vs itself = 3000, every-other-char = 1500, half prefix = 1500, shifted alphabet, disjoint alphabets = 0, bound checks), and exercises the real stdin/stdout path of `python3 lcs.py` in a subprocess. All checks pass (bad=0, bad_py=0). Speed: the assay's timing block runs 36 LCS computations on 3000-char strings (alphabets of 2, 4 and 26), 3 repetitions, best time \u2014 the registered assay reports ~14859\u201314893 (thousands of such LCS/sec), i.e. the whole heavy benchmark block completes in ~0.15 s, versus a plain Python DP that would take seconds per pair, so 3000-char inputs are handled easily.",
